# Parameter Categories

SilentRunner options are organized into logical groups. Most execution
scenarios require configuring only a small subset of these categories.

## Process Environment

Controls the execution environment and runtime behavior of the child
process and run hooks.

By default, the child process inherits SilentRunner's current working
directory, receives `NUL` as its standard input, uses the system's
default console code page, and runs without a time limit.

These options allow the working directory, standard input handling,
console code page, execution timeout, and runtime-failure abort behavior
to be customized.

### Working Directory

The configured working directory controls the working directory in which the
child process and run hooks execute. It does not change the base directory used
to resolve relative paths in SilentRunner path options; those paths are resolved
relative to SilentRunner's inherited working directory.

The directory specified by `--cwd` must already exist. SilentRunner does not
create it.

### Termination on Runtime Supervision Failure

A runtime supervision failure occurs when the infrastructure SilentRunner relies on to
supervise child execution through completion and obtain the main child's
exit code fails. It is distinct from an unsuccessful child execution or
an output-processing failure.

If runtime supervision fails, SilentRunner does not terminate the child
process or its managed process tree unless `--abort-on-runtime-failure`
is enabled. With that option, SilentRunner requests termination of the
managed process tree. Without it, completion may remain pending if
supervision is unavailable. A reported runtime supervision failure
produces runtime exit code `255`.

Runtime supervision failures are expected to be rare under normal operating
conditions, so `--abort-on-runtime-failure` is generally unnecessary. Enable
it only when terminating the managed process tree is preferable to leaving
it running after a supervision failure.

After successful process-tree termination, readers receive one shared
grace period of up to two seconds to finish naturally before cancellation.

Options:

-   `--cwd <dir>`
-   `--inherit-stdin`
-   `--utf8` or `--utf-8`
-   `--timeout-ms <ms>`
-   `--abort-on-runtime-failure`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Execution ID Configuration

Controls the identifier assigned to each execution.

The execution ID identifies an execution and forms the
base name of all log files created during that run.

The execution ID is composed as:

`id-prefix + id-base + id-suffix`

All three components are optional and may be used independently.

If none is specified, the execution ID defaults to `timestamp+pid` (UTC).

Options:

-   `--id-prefix <value>`
-   `--id-base <value>`
-   `--id-suffix <timestamp|pid|timestamp+pid|pid+timestamp>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Child Event Framing

Controls how child stdout and stderr byte streams are divided into individual
events before they enter the execution timeline.

Stdout and stderr framing are configured independently.

Supported framing modes:

-   `chunk` -- Create events from the underlying read chunks. This is the
    default.
-   `lf` or `newline` -- End an event when LF is encountered.
-   `crlf` -- End an event when an exact CRLF sequence is encountered.

For newline-based framing (`lf`, `newline`, or `crlf`), a maximum event size
may also be configured. If the configured newline sequence is not encountered
before this threshold is reached, the current event is completed and
a new event begins.

If a newline maximum is not specified, newline-based framing uses a default
threshold of 524288 bytes (512 KiB).

With `crlf` framing, the configured threshold is not a strict byte limit.
If the threshold is reached with a trailing CR, SilentRunner may consume one
additional byte to determine whether it completes a CRLF sequence.

The `*-event-newline-max-bytes` options must be greater than zero and are
valid only when the corresponding event framing is `lf`, `newline`, or `crlf`.

With newline-based framing, when the child stream ends, any remaining
bytes form the final event.

Options:

-   `--stdout-event-framing <chunk|lf|newline|crlf>`
-   `--stdout-event-newline-max-bytes <bytes>`
-   `--stderr-child-event-framing <chunk|lf|newline|crlf>`
-   `--stderr-child-event-newline-max-bytes <bytes>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Parent Emission

Parent emission determines which events are emitted to the parent process,
how they are presented, and when or whether they are emitted.

The option name specifies the event sources, output destination, event
filter, and presentation. The `<mode>` argument determines the emission
timing and condition.

For example, `--stderr-emit-child-raw <mode>` consists of:

~~~text
--stderr-emit-child-raw <mode>

stderr   → Event sources: child stderr and SilentRunner diagnostics
emit     → Output destination: parent stderr (corresponds to source channel)
child    → Event filter: pass only child stderr events
raw      → Presentation: Raw
<mode>   → Emission mode
~~~

The `child`, `sr`, and `incl-stdout` suffixes determine which events pass
the filter. Without the `-raw` suffix, Headered presentation is used.

In Headered presentation, child event framing determines how headers
are applied to child stdout/stderr events. Raw presentation preserves
the original child bytes without headers or event separators, regardless
of child event framing.

For details, see [Raw and Headered Presentation](architecture.md#raw-and-headered-presentation).

Emission modes:

-   `stream` -- Emit the selected events to the parent as they are produced (default).
-   `end` -- Emit the selected events during finalization.
-   `success` -- Emit the selected events only if execution succeeds.
-   `failure` -- Emit the selected events only if execution fails.

-   `never` -- Never emit the selected output to the parent.
    With `--stderr-emit never`, this disables the default SR diagnostic channel.

For details on delayed emission (`end`, `success`, and `failure`) and replay sources, see [Buffering](#buffering).

Options:

-   `--stdout-emit <mode>` -- Child stdout (Headered).
-   `--stdout-emit-raw <mode>` -- Child stdout (Raw).
-   `--stderr-emit <mode>` -- Child stderr and SilentRunner diagnostics (Headered).
-   `--stderr-emit-child <mode>` -- Child stderr (Headered).
-   `--stderr-emit-child-raw <mode>` -- Child stderr (Raw).
-   `--stderr-emit-sr <mode>` -- SilentRunner diagnostics (Headered).
-   `--stderr-emit-incl-stdout <mode>` -- Child stdout, child stderr,
    and SilentRunner diagnostics (Headered).

`--stdout-emit` and `--stdout-emit-raw` are mutually exclusive.
The five stderr emit options are mutually exclusive.

Raw child-stderr parent emission contains only child stderr bytes, not
SilentRunner diagnostics.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Persistent Logging

Persistent logging determines which events are written to per-execution
log files, how they are presented, and where those files are stored.

The option name specifies the event sources, output destination, event
filter, and presentation. The `<dir>` argument specifies the output directory.

For example, `--stderr-dir-child-raw <dir>` consists of:

~~~text
--stderr-dir-child-raw <dir>

stderr   → Event sources: child stderr and SilentRunner diagnostics
dir      → Output destination: directory containing per-execution log files
child    → Event filter: pass only child stderr events
raw      → Presentation: Raw (TXT format)
<dir>    → Output directory path

Alternative presentation suffix:
jsonl    → Presentation: JSON record (JSONL format)
~~~

The `child`, `sr`, and `incl-stdout` suffixes determine which events pass
the filter. Without `-raw` or `-jsonl`, Headered presentation (TXT format)
is used.

Headered TXT logs apply headers according to child event framing,
as described in [Raw and Headered Presentation](architecture.md#raw-and-headered-presentation).
Raw TXT logs preserve the original child bytes without headers or event
separators, regardless of child event framing. JSONL logs use JSON record
presentation, with one JSON record containing metadata per event.

The `--jsonl-payload-representation` option controls how event payloads are
represented in JSONL logs. The default is text.

-   `text` -- Write the payload as text.
-   `base64` -- Write the payload as Base64.
-   `text+base64` or `base64+text` -- Write both text and Base64
    representations. The two values are equivalent.

When a child stdout or stderr payload is not valid UTF-8, a requested text
representation automatically falls back to Base64 so that the original payload
bytes can be preserved without producing invalid JSON text.

Persistent logging is independent of parent stdout/stderr emission.
All log destinations may be enabled simultaneously. For example,
child stdout may be streamed to the parent while also being recorded
in Raw TXT, Headered TXT, and JSONL log files. Child stderr and
SilentRunner diagnostics can be logged separately, together, or as
part of a combined chronological output including child stdout.

When persistent logging is enabled, each execution creates its own set of log
files. Log files are not reused across executions, and existing target
filenames cause an error.

Log filenames are derived from the execution ID, as described in
[Execution ID](execution.md#execution-id).

Log files initially use the `running` suffix. During log finalization,
retained files are renamed with a `success` or `failure` suffix based on
the execution result determined at that point. See [Exit Codes](execution.md#exit-codes)
for finalization behavior.
For example: `my-execution_stdout_running.log` →
`my-execution_stdout_success.log` or `my-execution_stdout_failure.log`.

Options:

-   `--stdout-dir <dir>`
-   `--stdout-dir-jsonl <dir>`
-   `--stdout-dir-raw <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-jsonl <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-child <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-child-jsonl <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-child-raw <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-sr <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-sr-jsonl <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-incl-stdout <dir>`
-   `--stderr-dir-incl-stdout-jsonl <dir>`
-   `--jsonl-payload-representation <text|base64|text+base64|base64+text>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Log Retention

Controls whether persistent log files are retained during finalization.

Log retention is configured independently for each event filter,
regardless of presentation.

For example, `--stderr-dir-child-keep-log` applies to all enabled
child stderr logs (Raw TXT, Headered TXT, and JSONL).

Logs removed by a retention policy are deleted permanently and are not
moved to the Windows Recycle Bin.

Supported modes:

-   `always` -- Always keep the log file (default).
-   `success` -- Keep the log file only if execution succeeds.
-   `failure` -- Keep the log file only if execution fails.

Options:

-   `--stdout-dir-keep-log <mode>`
-   `--stderr-dir-keep-log <mode>`
-   `--stderr-dir-child-keep-log <mode>`
-   `--stderr-dir-sr-keep-log <mode>`
-   `--stderr-dir-incl-stdout-keep-log <mode>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Buffering

The execution timeline retains event entries, including their metadata,
payloads, and per-target job results, as long as they are needed.
Completed entries are pruned when their processing results allow it
and they are no longer required for processing or replay.

Delayed parent emission (`end`, `success`, or `failure` modes) requires
output to remain available until the configured replay condition is met.
SilentRunner supports two replay sources: persistent log files and the
in-memory execution timeline.

When compatible persistent log files are available, SilentRunner can
replay output from those files instead of retaining the corresponding
payloads in memory. The following table lists compatible log sources
in order of preference:

| Parent emission | Preferred log source | Alternative log source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--stdout-emit` | `--stdout-dir-jsonl` | `--stdout-dir` |
| `--stderr-emit` | `--stderr-dir-jsonl` | `--stderr-dir` |
| `--stderr-emit-child` | `--stderr-dir-child-jsonl` | `--stderr-dir-child` |
| `--stderr-emit-sr` | `--stderr-dir-sr-jsonl` | `--stderr-dir-sr` |
| `--stderr-emit-incl-stdout` | `--stderr-dir-incl-stdout-jsonl` | `--stderr-dir-incl-stdout` |
| `--stdout-emit-raw` | `--stdout-dir-raw` | — |
| `--stderr-emit-child-raw` | `--stderr-dir-child-raw` | — |

Delayed Raw parent emission uses the corresponding Raw TXT log file
when available; otherwise, it falls back to in-memory timeline replay,
subject to replay buffer limits. Headered TXT and JSONL logs cannot
serve as Raw replay sources.

Persistent replay reads log files incrementally, but the amount of data
temporarily retained in memory depends on the replay source and the original
child event framing.

| Replay source | Child event framing | Event reconstruction | Memory behavior |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Raw TXT | Any | Original child bytes are replayed without reconstructing events or metadata. | Read and replayed incrementally. |
| Headered TXT | `chunk` | Consecutive child events of the same type (stdout or stderr) may share one block; their original event boundaries cannot be reconstructed. | Large blocks are processed incrementally and split into smaller replay payloads. |
| Headered TXT | `lf` / `crlf` | Reconstructs individual child events and their metadata. | The whole event must be loaded into memory before replay. |
| JSONL | Any | Each JSON record preserves an individual event and its metadata. | The whole JSON record must be loaded into memory before replay. |
| Execution timeline | Any | Reuses retained timeline events without file-based reconstruction. | Events are already retained in memory when required for replay. |

**Large line events:** With `lf` or `crlf` framing, very long lines
can produce large events. To limit their size, use the following options
to split long lines into multiple events:
- `--stdout-event-newline-max-bytes`
- `--stderr-child-event-newline-max-bytes`

When replay uses the execution timeline instead, the necessary event
payloads are retained until they can be replayed to the parent process.
Replay buffer limits control their memory usage. Exceeding any configured
limit aborts execution with exit code 125. By default, all limits are
0 (unlimited).
Options:

- `--stdout-max-buffer-bytes <bytes>`
- `--stderr-max-buffer-bytes <bytes>`
- `--std-total-max-buffer-bytes <bytes>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Post-Execution Hooks

Runs an external program during finalization without arguments.

Hooks execute in the same process environment as the child process,
including the configured working directory and environment variables.
The hook path is resolved during Prepare relative to SilentRunner's inherited
working directory, not relative to the directory specified by `--cwd`.

SilentRunner also provides additional execution-specific environment
variables, allowing hooks to access execution metadata such as the
execution ID, execution result, and log file locations.

`SILENTRUNNER_ABORTED_ON_RUNTIME_FAILURE` is `true` when a runtime failure
triggered the optional abort action, otherwise `false`. The value indicates
that an abort was initiated, not that process-tree termination succeeded.

`SILENTRUNNER_OUTPUT_WRITE_FAILURES` reports worker output-write failures as
`outputWriteFailures_N=[Target_GLE=number,...]`, or
`outputWriteFailures_0=[]` when none were recorded. The hook value omits
textual reasons and system error messages, which remain in diagnostics.

The complete list of currently available environment variables can be displayed
using `SilentRunner.exe --help`. Requests for additional environment variables are
welcome.

Post-execution hooks are started as detached processes without inherited
standard handles. The hook and programs started by it therefore cannot
rely on the original SilentRunner parent stdout or stderr handles.

If SilentRunner cannot prepare or launch the selected hook, it returns
exit code `253`. A successfully created hook process is not monitored;
subsequent hook failures do not change SilentRunner's exit code.

Final log-path environment variables contain paths only for logs that were
successfully finalized and retained. If a log was removed by its retention
policy or could not be renamed to its final filename, the corresponding
variable is empty.

When a hook starts another SilentRunner instance, a persistent diagnostic
log (for example, `--stderr-dir`) is recommended because parent stderr
is not inherited.

Options:

-   `--run-on-success <path>`
-   `--run-on-failure <path>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Diagnostics

Controls the level of SilentRunner diagnostics.

Informational, error, and fatal diagnostics are enabled by default.

During startup, SilentRunner first parses its command-line arguments to
determine the requested output destinations, then initializes the diagnostic
output infrastructure. Until this infrastructure is ready, startup errors
are reported directly to parent stderr on a best-effort basis. These messages
are not written to persistent logs; if parent stderr is unavailable, the
exit code may be the only indication of failure.

Once initialized, SilentRunner diagnostics follow the configured parent
emission and persistent logging options. They may be routed independently,
together with child stderr, or as part of the combined chronological output
including child stdout.

SilentRunner can run without an available diagnostic output destination.
In that case, diagnostic messages may not be observable.

The `--debug` option enables additional diagnostic messages describing
internal execution flow, child (sub)process lifecycle, and output
routing decisions.

When available, IOCP process lifecycle diagnostics include exit codes for
individual tracked processes. These codes are provided for diagnostic
purposes and do not change SilentRunner's overall exit code, which normally
comes from the main child process.

With `--debug` or `--verbose`, Job Object process notifications (IOCP) must
initialize successfully; otherwise Runtime returns `255` before the child
starts. Without debug diagnostics, execution can continue without these
notifications.
The `--verbose` option implies `--debug` and adds detailed execution
summaries, including event processing, worker activity, and final
processing results.

The `--probe-dir` option enables additional development-oriented diagnostics.
Its output format may change between versions.
Options:

-   `--debug`
-   `--verbose`
-   `--probe-dir <dir>`

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Help

Displays the built-in CLI reference.

Options:

-   `--help`
